WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Good morning Hank; it's Friday.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:02.440
I woke up last Sunday in Bruges.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:03.440
Uughh.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.649
Hmm. I wonder what happened the night before.

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:13.910
What starts with L and rhymes with mistake?
...Let's have another beer... take.

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.080
Got up anyway, took a shower, brushed my teeth,
got dressed- wait, why aren't you brushing

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.080
your teeth?

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.750
Cause I left my toothbrush in Amsterdam.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.060
Right, so I headed out into Bruges with a
burning need to disregard currency and acquire

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:27.810
one, a toothbrush, and two, some of that fancy
Belgian chocolate I'd heard so much about.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.869
Fortunately, Bruges has a lot of stores; unfortunately
most of them are closed on Sunday mornings,

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:35.160
and I was starting to worry that I was going
to have to do the non-ideal pre-modern thing

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.140
of brushing one's teeth with one's finger
when ah look! Chocolate! The woman wrapped

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.500
up my chocolate for me in a fancy box as if
I was not going to eat it right away.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:50.960
Did you know Hank, that the ancient Belgians
used chocolate as a way of staving off bad

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.810
breath... is a fact I just made up?

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.700
I found a lot of places that didn't sell toothbrushes
like the french fry museum and a fancy shoe

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.550
store and a book store with John Green books
and the Concept Store which presumably only

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.009
sells concepts; I wanted to buy utilitarianism,
but it was closed.

00:01:04.009 --> 00:01:08.560
I kept walking until at last I found Sam &amp; Co,
uw buurtwinkel. Hank, I know from my extensive

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.829
knowledge of Dutch that "winkel" means "store";
I could only hope that "buurt" meant "toothbrush"...

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:17.749
and it did. It did occur to me that I paid
two euros for something that if you gave me

00:01:17.749 --> 00:01:20.220
one hundred years, I could not build from
scratch.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:24.590
Hank, I realize this is stating the obvious,
but everything in Bruges is really fracking

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:31.359
old. This is a school. And this is a Domino's.
Parts of Bruges's famous belfry date back

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:36.960
to 1240 meaning that this building is four
hundred years older than the idea of gravity.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.509
I heard a lot of people call Bruges "quaint"
but while it did feel like an open-air history

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:45.170
museum to me, there's nothing quaint about
living amid history, grappling with the gifts

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.329
and insufficiencies of your ancestors.

00:01:47.329 --> 00:01:50.950
To live in Bruges is to be reminded every
day that there were people before you were

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.360
a person, which of course also reminds you
that there will be people after. I think we

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.840
owe both the dead and the not-yet-living precisely
the same thing-- the daily awareness that

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.649
human life is ours only in trust.

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:05.970
It's worth remembering that seven hundred
years later we don't remember the individuals

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:10.340
who built the belfry or dug the canals; we
don't remember their political affiliations

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:15.209
or even their nationalistic identities; we
remember them as a collective, just as we

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:21.420
ourselves will be remembered as a collective
for what we did and failed to do together.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.189
Late I found myself in a park with Nerdfighters
from all over Belgium, grateful to be part

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:30.040
of a community that builds friendships while
trying to make the world suck less.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.900
In short, Hank, I felt grateful to the 13th
century, but lucky to be alive in 2011, living

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:38.970
in a world where with a series of ones and
zeros, we can build our own relationships

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:43.090
and our own monuments. Plus, toothbrushes
are crazy cheap!

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.129
Hank, I'll see you on Monday.

